    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d685">

                <pb facs="0903" n="892" sameAs="frus:frus1937v02/pg892"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867N.01/775: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Beirut</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Marriner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-09T12:00:00+02:00">July 9, 1937—noon.</date><lb/>[Received
                        2:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Arabic opinion as expressed in the press here and in Damascus is in general
                    dissatisfied with Royal Commission report on Palestine<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d685-f1"/> stating that the “surgical operation” proposed by Great
                    Britain is destined to create confusion, to leave all power on the divide and
                    rule principle to England, and effectively to cut the Arabs away from the coast
                    line and all the richer lands. At the request of the British, the general
                    commanding here assures me that strict measures have been taken for guarding the
                    Palestine frontier to prevent any armed bands from Syria crossing to assist in
                    possible demonstrations. The intention attributed to the Nationalist Party of
                    preparing and arming such bands, allegedly at the request of the Grand
                        Mufti<anchor n="17" xml:id="d685-f2"/> of Jerusalem who recently visited
                    Damascus, has been categorically denied by Nationalist leaders. They state that
                    while they sympathize with the Arabs in Palestine there can be no question of
                    armed intervention.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Marriner</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d685-f1" n="16" xml:id="d685-f1-content">
                <p>British Cmd. 5479: <hi rendition="#italic">Palestine, Royal Commission
                        Report</hi>, July, 1937.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d685-f2" n="17" xml:id="d685-f2-content">
                <p>Haj Amin Efendi al–Husseini.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
